.TH NEWGRP 1 
.SH NAME
newgrp \- log in to a new group
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B newgrp
group
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Newgrp
changes the group identification of its caller,
analogously to
.IR  login (1).
The same person remains logged in,
and the current directory is unchanged,
but calculations of access permissions to files are
performed with respect to the
new group ID.
.PP
A password is demanded if the group has
a password and the user himself does not.
.PP
When most users log in, they
are members of the group named `other.'
.PP
.B Note:
the caller must be listed among the group's members in
.IR group (5),
with one exception:
the group literally named `other' is exempt from that test, which is
what the paragraph above is really about.
On this system `other' is group 1 and the one ordinary account,
.BR guest ,
logs in as group 3.
.PP
.B Note:
.I newgrp
is
.I not
known to this shell, and
.IR sh (1)
forks for it like any other command.
The consequence is that the new group lasts only until that shell is
left, and that the login shell underneath it is unaffected.
.PP
.B Note:
every outcome, including every refusal, ends by executing a shell.
.I Newgrp
has replaced nothing at that point \- the shell that forked it is still
waiting \- but a refusal that simply exited would be indistinguishable
from one that worked, and on a login shell it would end the session.
So `Sorry' or `no such group' is followed by a prompt, with the group
unchanged.
The exit status cannot report any of this and does not try.
.PP
.B Note:
.I newgrp
is set-user-id to root, and narrowly:
.IR setgid (2)
admits a group the caller is already in, or the super-user, and the
whole point of the command is the case that is neither.
The privilege is given back with
.IR setuid (2)
before the shell is executed.
.SH FILES
/etc/group, /etc/passwd
.SH "SEE ALSO"
login(1), su(1), passwd(1), group(5), setuid(2)
